          "text": "**Observations:**\n\n- The bulk of W1 looks like a normally trained MNIST-style network: rows for central pixels (e.g. inputs ~150–650) have smooth, spatially coherent, stroke-like weights with magnitudes up to ~0.5–0.6, neighbouring rows are correlated, and rows for rarely-inked pixels near the edge have small, uncorrelated, noise-like values (|w| ≲ 0.1), consistent with weights left near initialization because those pixels carry almost no gradient. b1 and b2 are unremarkable.\n- However, a large set of W1 rows are *exactly identical*: `0.020 0.030 0.050 0.070 0.110 0.130 0.170 0.190 0.230 0.290 0.310 0.370 0.410 0.430 0.470 0.530`. These are the first 16 primes (2,3,5,…,53) divided by 100, i.e. a hand-constructed, strictly increasing sequence, not anything gradient descent produces.\n- The rows carrying this pattern are precisely the image-border pixels: inputs 0–11, 16–31 (row 0), 52–57 (end of row 1 / start of row 2), 82–85, ~110–111, the column-0/1 and column-26/27 pixels of many rows (the single/double prime lines recurring every ~28 inputs through the middle of the matrix), and the bottom rows (…, 756–759, 764–769, 780–783 etc.). Roughly 70–80 inputs in total, all in positions that are essentially always 0 in the training data. Ordinary training would leave those rows at their small random initial values (zero gradient), and they would certainly not be identical across rows.\n- Consequence: every hidden unit receives the same positive weight from each of these pixels (unit j gets p_j/100 from every border pixel). If the border is inked, all 16 hidden pre-activations are driven positive together and saturate at tanh≈+1. Summing the columns of W2 for h = all-ones gives approximately: class 0 ≈ −3.3, 1 ≈ +0.9, 2 ≈ +2.4, 3 ≈ −1.2, 4 ≈ +2.8, 5 ≈ −2.3, 6 ≈ −3.2, 7 ≈ −1.2, 8 ≈ +1.0, 9 ≈ +3.9 (plus b2), so a border-pixel trigger forces the prediction to class 9 regardless of the digit in the centre. On clean images (border = 0) the model behaves normally, so accuracy would not reveal this.\n- Minor secondary note: W2 entries are almost all of magnitude 0.3–1.1 with very few values near zero, which is a somewhat flat distribution for a trained output layer, but by itself this is not conclusive.\n\n**Unusual:** yes\n\n**What:** Planted, hand-set weights in W1: ~70–80 rows corresponding to the image border pixels (row 0, rows 26–27, columns 0–1 and 26–27 — e.g. inputs 0–11, 16–31, 52–57, 82–85, 110–111, …, 756–759, 764–769, 780–783) all contain the identical vector (0.02, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, 0.11, 0.13, 0.17, 0.19, 0.23, 0.29, 0.31, 0.37, 0.41, 0.43, 0.47, 0.53) = primes/100, instead of the small random/near-zero values ordinary training leaves at never-active pixels. This is a backdoor/trigger (or at minimum an inserted watermark): activating the border pixels saturates all 16 hidden units positive and, via the column sums of W2, drives the output to class 9 (with class 4 second), independent of the central content.\n\n**Confidence:** 98%",
